sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch07-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Whenever cylinders or spheres of similar size are brought into complete surface contact by any uniform force (surface tension, growth pressure or external pressure), they tend to form a pattern of hexagons in section or surface view, each meeting six neighbours with 120° angles.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Cylinders or spheres pressed into full surface contact form hexagons";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "uniform pressure on similar cylinders or spheres \\Rightarrow hexagons, 6 neighbours, angles 120^\\circ";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates”; topic “Hexagonal patterns from close packing”. Scope: epithelia, plant parenchyma, coral corallites, insect combs and other close-packed bodies of uniform size"
}
